course: everyday
lesson: 449
title: Stories that change
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Josh came home with a bump on his head. «Fell off the swing.» An hour later: «Tripped on a stair.» At dinner: «Leo pushed me.» His mum noticed the story had changed three times. She didn't shout; she asked gently, «What really happened, love?» Josh had been in a scuffle and was scared to say. A true story stays more or less the same each time it's told. A made-up one has to be built again every time, and it comes out a bit different.
explain: Stories can also patch themselves when they're challenged. A boy in Sydney said his dad «drove him to school». His teacher had seen him walking. «Oh — he dropped me at the corner.» New details that appear only after a problem are a clue. Sophie said she'd been «at the library till 6». Mum asked, «What time does it close?» «Er… 6?» It closes at 5. One fact that can be looked up — opening times, bus times, the weather — can test a whole story.
explain: A gentle question can show whether someone was really there. Emma's friend said she'd seen the new Pixar film. Emma said «I saw it too — wasn't the ending sad?» «Yeah, so sad.» The ending was happy. Emma didn't say anything; she just knew. In Cork, a teacher asked two boys separately where they'd been at lunch. Both said «the library, together». She asked what book. One said «a dinosaur one», the other «a football one». They'd been behind the bike shed, and laughed about the detention later. Two kids with a plan agree on the big things but not on the small ones.
explain: Some stories just don't fit the world. Tom's little brother said the dog ate the cake — but the dog was locked in the garden, and the brother had chocolate on his chin. Everyone laughed; the dog looked insulted. Ollie said he'd read every night; his log was all ticked in the same pen at the same angle, done in one go on Friday morning. Real life is messy; things done over days look different. And Grace's friend said «I didn't eat your sweets, I swear on my mum's life, I'd never, honestly, I promise» — then Grace found the wrapper in her bag. People telling the truth usually just say «No, I didn't.» A pile of swearing for a small thing can be a sign.
explain: Not every changing story is a lie. Kai's mate swore he'd seen a shark at the beach. Then it was a dolphin. Then «a big fish thingy». Kai said «Yeah nah, mate, sounds like a stick» — in Australia «yeah nah» means no — and they both cracked up. His mate wasn't lying; he just wasn't sure what he'd seen. When two kids blamed each other for a push — «by the gate!», «by the bins!» — the teacher asked a third kid: «They both pushed, by the bench.» When stories clash, ask what people are sure of, and find someone else who saw.
fact: A true story usually stays much the same each time it is told; a made-up one often changes.
fact: Details that appear only after someone is challenged can be a sign that a story is being patched.
fact: One simple checkable fact, like opening times, can test a whole story.
fact: A gentle question about a detail can show whether someone was really there.
fact: Asking two people the same small questions separately shows whether their stories really match.
fact: Ask whether a story could have happened that way; if it couldn't, it didn't.
fact: Evidence that looks too neat, all done at once, can show something was faked at the last minute.
fact: Too much swearing and promising about a small thing can be a sign of lying; truthful people often simply say «No».
fact: Not every changing story is a lie; people can be unsure, and their story shrinks when they are doubted.
fact: When two stories clash, a witness who saw it from outside can help settle it.
fact: Checking a story works best done kindly and calmly, not to embarrass someone.
fact: «Yeah nah» (Australia, New Zealand) means no; «cracked up» means burst out laughing; a «scuffle» is a small fight.
exercise: How many times did Josh's story about the bump change? | Three times | Once | Twice | Four times
exercise: What did Josh's mum do when the story kept changing? | Asked gently what really happened | Shouted that he was a liar | Believed the last story he told, because it was the newest | Rang Leo's mum straight away
exercise: Why do made-up stories often change? | They have to be built again each time | True stories are always shorter than made-up ones | People forget true things faster | Stories change with the weather
exercise: What did the boy in Sydney add after his teacher saw him walking? | «He dropped me at the corner» | «I walked because I like walking» | «I was walking home with my dad» | «It was my twin brother you saw»
exercise: How did Sophie's mum test the library story? | She asked when the library closes | She rang the librarian at home that night | She looked at Sophie's books | She asked Sophie's friend
exercise: What question showed Emma her friend hadn't seen the film? | «Wasn't the ending sad?» — it was happy | «Did you like the popcorn and the drinks there?» | «Which cinema did you go to?» | «Who did you go with?»
exercise: How did the Cork teacher catch the two boys? | She asked each one separately about the book | She saw them behind the shed herself, from the staff room window | The librarian told on them | She looked at the library cards
exercise: Why couldn't the dog have eaten the cake? | It was locked in the garden | Dogs don't like cake | The cake was too high up on the shelf | The dog was asleep
exercise: What gave Tom's brother away? | Chocolate on his chin | A crumb on the dog | A note he wrote | The empty plate
exercise: What was odd about Ollie's reading log? | Every tick was the same, done in one go | The log was completely empty | The ticks were in many different colours of pen | The book was too short
exercise: Why was Grace's friend's long promise suspicious? | Too much swearing for a small thing | Because she said «no» simply | Because she offered to share the crisps with everyone | Because she cried
exercise: Was Kai's mate lying about the shark? | Probably not — he just wasn't sure | Yes, he lied on purpose | Yes, he never even went to the beach that day | No, it really was a shark
exercise: Two kids' stories clash: «by the gate» and «by the bins». What helps? | Ask a third kid who saw | Believe the one who shouts louder | Believe the older one, because older kids know best | Punish both and forget it
exercise: What do truthful people usually say when accused of something small? | «No, I didn't.» | «I swear on my life, ever, honestly.» | «Prove it or else!» | «Why would you ask me?»
exercise: What does «cracked up» mean? | Burst out laughing | Broke into pieces | Got very angry | Fell over
test: Little bro: «dog ate the cake». Dog's been outside all day. Bro has chocolate on his face. What happened? | The brother ate it | The dog climbed in a window | The cake melted away | Someone else broke in
test: Mate in Sydney: «saw a shark — nah, a dolphin — well, a big fish thingy». Most likely? | He's not sure what he saw | He's telling a careful lie | It changed into a dolphin underwater | Sharks look just like dolphins
test: «Library till 6!» It shuts at 5. What would happen if Sophie said where she really was? | Less trouble than being caught lying | Much more trouble than now | Mum would never ask about the library again | The library would stay open
test: Kid: «I didn't, I swear on my nan's life, honest, I'd never, I promise!!» What does all that swearing suggest? | It may be a sign of lying | It proves she's honest | She's very religious | She's just being polite
test: Teacher: «You were at the library? Both of you? Which book?» Why ask them separately? | Real memories match on small details | To make them miss their lunch as a punishment | So they can't hear the answer | Because teachers like books
test: Your friend says «a story that changes is ALWAYS a lie». Is she right? | No — sometimes people are unsure | Yes, every single time | Yes, but only when they are at school | No, stories never change
test: Josh: «fell off the swing — no, the stairs — no, Leo pushed me». What's the kind next step? | Ask gently what really happened | Laugh at him for lying so badly | Ground him for a month without asking anything | Believe the first story he told
test: Reading log, every tick the same pen, same slant. Mum: «You doing a detective job on me?» What gave it away? | It was too neat, done all at once | It had no ticks at all, not even one | It was done in pencil | It was the wrong book
test: In a TV detective show, the liar always sweats and stammers. In real life what's a better clue? | Details that change or don't fit the facts | People who sweat are lying | Stammering or going red always means someone is lying | Liars always look at the floor
test: «He pushed me by the gate!» «No, he pushed ME by the bins!» What would settle it best? | Someone who saw it from outside | Whoever cries first | Whoever is taller | A coin toss
test: Friend: «yeah the film was soooo sad at the end». It ended happily. What does Emma learn? | Her friend probably didn't see it | Her friend has a bad memory for films | The film has two endings | Emma remembered it wrong
exam: Identify the principal characteristic by which a fabricated account may frequently be distinguished from a truthful one. | Its details alter upon each repetition | It is invariably longer than a truthful account | It is invariably delivered with a smile | It contains no details whatsoever
exam: It is given that a pupil claims to have remained at a library until six o'clock, whereas the library closes at five. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The claim is contradicted by a single verifiable fact | The claim is confirmed by the closing time | The closing time is irrelevant to the claim | The library must have remained open for the pupil
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an account that alters as the speaker becomes uncertain? | It may reflect genuine uncertainty rather than deliberate falsehood | It is invariably a deliberate falsehood, the beach being a familiar place | It is invariably entirely accurate | It cannot be examined further
exam: Determine the purpose of questioning two persons separately as to minor particulars of a shared event. | To ascertain whether their recollections agree in detail | To prevent them from eating lunch together | To punish both persons equally | To discover which of them is older
